== The Epistle Dedicatory

Right Honorable, among all the societies and states of which the whole world of mankind has consisted, from the first calling of Adam in Paradise to this day, the first and most ancient is the family. For look into the Scriptures. The writings of Moses, which in time go beyond all the histories and records of men, plainly declare that it was the will of God to sanctify that first joining of Adam and Eve as the root in which man’s whole posterity was contained #emph[in virtue and power,] and from which, in the ages that followed, both church and commonwealth were to spring and grow to their perfection. And all those nations and countries before the Flood, whose heads descended in a direct line from the first two houses of Seth and Cain, had no outward form of civil government by which they were ruled, nor any visible face of a church to which they belonged. The whole frame of their government, both civil and ecclesiastical, was confined within the bounds of private families. And from the Flood onward, the house of Noah became the common mother in which the other two states were contained, and out of whose womb they issued afterward as posterity multiplied, in number equal to the stars of the sky and the sand on the seashore.

The verdict of the heathen on this point has answered to the voice of Scripture. Some of the#footnote[Aristot. Politic. 1.] learned among them have called the family#footnote[Xenoph. in Oeconom.] #emph[the first society in nature, and the ground] of all the rest. Others again have compared it to the beehive, which we call the stock, in which many swarms are bred that fly out from it into the world, to raise and maintain other states.#footnote[Xenoph. ibid.] Others, not unfitly, liken it to a metropolis, or mother city, which first trains up her native inhabitants and then#footnote[ἀποικίζει [sends out to settle elsewhere]. Cic. 3. de Nat. Deor.] moves some of them to other places to live, where they may be formed as members, to live in obedience to the laws of their head.

Since the family is in this way the seedbed of all other societies, it follows that its holy and righteous government is a direct means to the good ordering of both church and commonwealth. Yes, its laws, rightly informed and religiously observed, help to prepare and dispose men to keep order in other governments. That is why the Holy Spirit, in the book of the Scriptures, has in great wisdom commended both rules for direction and examples for imitation to husbands and wives, to parents and children, to masters and servants, in every point of Christian conduct toward God and man. For this first society is, as it were, the school in which the principles of authority and subjection are taught and learned. And just as the superior who fails in his private charge will prove unfit for public employment,#footnote[1 Tim 3:5] so the inferior who has not been trained in a course of subjection in the household will hardly bear the yoke of civil obedience.

Again, the corruption or decline of this first government must of necessity lead to the ruin of the rest. For an error in the foundation puts the body and every part of the whole building in plain danger. In the old world, before the Flood,#footnote[Gen 6:12.] though “all flesh had corrupted their ways,” yet the first source of that universal flood of sin came out of the household state, and from there it spread into the whole earth. It was the fault of the private government of Eli the priest that turned his sons Hophni and Phinehas#footnote[1 Sam 2:12, 23, 24, 25.] from graceless children into ungodly churchmen. It was David’s error in the running and guidance of his own house#footnote[2 Sam 14:33.] that encouraged his sons Absalom and Adonijah,#footnote[1 Kgs 1:5, 6.] presuming on their father’s indulgence,#footnote[2 Sam 15] to become bad citizens of the commonwealth,#footnote[1 Kgs 1.] and to aspire to the kingdom and usurp it by plots and practices of rebellion.

To forbear further instances of this kind: from due consideration of what has been said, I hold it both necessary and profitable for people of every rank to be thoroughly informed of the right way of setting up and ordering the private state of the family, so that they may be furnished with fit grounds for the common good in more public and open courses. For this end the present discourse of Christian Economy was first composed by the author, and it is now thought fit to be published for the instruction and edification of the multitude. It commends itself by its shortness and clearness, both of which, applied to the understanding and the memory, may give satisfaction to every well-disposed reader. And it also has a kind of eminence above other treatises of this nature published before, in various respects.

First, experience teaches that the questions of conscience in matters of marriage are very many in number and difficult in nature. Here we will find either direct answers to some of them, or at least the grounds for resolving various cases, warranted by the word, in such a way that, on trial, little or nothing that bears on the reader’s satisfaction will appear to be left untouched.

Secondly, the main aim of the author in its several branches is to make plain this truth in particular: that no family can have a share in the blessing and favor of God which is not founded in his fear and ordered according to his revealed will. It is true that the heathen, in their writings on the same subject,#footnote[Aristot. Politic. Xenoph. in Oecon.] show that nature by her dim light may give some good directions to this purpose, and that merely natural men have prospered in practicing the virtues of household government. Yet the true happiness of houses and their inhabitants depends wholly on the special grace of God, and comes out of his promise; and that promise is directed only to those “who fear the #smallcaps[Lord], who walk in His ways,”#footnote[Ps 128:1] for it is godliness alone that holds “promise for the present life and for the one to come.”#footnote[1 Tim 4:8] Therefore to dream of a blessing in any other state that is not seasoned with godliness and ordered by the direction of divine law is only witless presumption. For the family itself, from which they grow, has no further assurance of blessedness than it has right and title in the promise.

Lastly, it contains here and there some special grounds of truth that serve to uncover various errors of popish doctrine in points belonging to marriage, namely these:

#set enum(numbering: "I.")

+ That this state is free to all men, even to those who have the gift of self-control; and for those who do not have the power to abstain, it is expressly enjoined by God as necessary.#footnote[1 Cor 7:1, 2, 8, 9.] This uncovers the error of forbidding it absolutely to some sorts of men, without the least exception made for their ability to abstain. And in this case, though the Church of Rome prescribes help by prayer and effort where the gift is lacking, yet here she adds two more absurdities to the former. She imposes by her laws a necessity of abstaining where God has given liberty by his word; and she makes men flee from the only remedy that he himself has sanctified to another, which he neither commands by his will nor encourages by promise.
+ That marriages consummated without the free and advised consent of parents, either #emph[explicit] in words or #emph[implicit] by tacit allowance, are mere nullities in the court of conscience. This doctrine is cleared in this book both by Scriptures and by the consent of antiquity. In it the Jesuits’ proposition is challenged, that the consent of the parties alone is sufficient#footnote[Bellarm. de Matrim. Sacram. libr. c. 19.] #emph[ad Matrimonium firmum ac ratum efficiendum] [to make a marriage firm and ratified]. So also is that decree of the Council of Trent#footnote[Concil. Trident. Sess. 24. Decreto de clandest. Matrim.] which professes to detest clandestine contracts, and yet in the same breath pronounces the curse of anathema on those who affirm that parents may ratify or make void marriages contracted by their children without their allowance.
+ That marriage lawfully begun and consummated is made void only by way of divorce in the case of adultery. So says our Savior:#footnote[Matt 5:32.] “anyone who divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality,” etc. And this liberty granted by Christ, both to dissolve the marriage and to marry again, cannot be restrained or cut off by any human ordinance. This point is directly attacked by two other rules of that council. One is that the Church of Rome does not err when she teaches (as they profess,#footnote[Concil. Trid. Sess. 24. Can. 7.] #emph[Iuxta Euangelicam & Apostolicam doctrinam] [according to the doctrine of the Gospel and the apostles], but in truth in express contradiction to it) that the knot of marriage cannot be broken because of adultery, and that neither party may undertake to marry again during the life of the other after the putting away. The other is that the vow of chastity and entering into holy orders has force to loose the bond of marriage. And in this case separation is made and allowed #emph[de facto] [in fact] by the sole authority of the Pope, even though the party who stays settled in the marriage refuses to consent.
+ That the judgment and determination of matrimonial causes (contracts and their impediments, degrees of kindred, divorces, and the like) rests #emph[originally] in the civil magistrate. It is in his power to ordain and establish laws concerning them, agreeable to God’s word, and to have them put into execution. For this purpose, in doubtful and difficult cases, the#footnote[#emph[See the] Code #emph[in various places.] Lib. 5. tit. 4 de Nuptijs, tit. 5. de incesto, etc.] ancient church did not rest absolutely on her own decrees, but had recourse to the seat of justice, especially under Christian magistrates, submitting herself to their laws and constitutions in that kind. The#footnote[Concil. Trid. sess. 24. can. 12.] Church of Rome, by contrast, has determined that these causes belong simply and only to ecclesiastical judges. And therefore she has concluded that marriage is a sacrament, so that the deciding of marriage cases, becoming on this ground an action of a spiritual nature, might be transferred from the civil courts to their spiritual courts. By their own constitutions, the sovereignty of those courts in these affairs is greatly enlarged. For though they admit that civil princes have some interest in them, yet the princes must hold it#footnote[Bellarm. de Matrim. Sacram. cap. 32.] #emph[in subordination to the authority of the church magistrate;] and when the governor of a province makes a law on this, he must do it #emph[approbante Principe Ecclesiastico,] by the allowance of the Pope; otherwise his laws are mere nullities. And among their reasons of policy, one of the chief is this: that by this means they might raise a profit for the See of Rome by the sale of their dispensations.

Now these points, together with the substance of this small discourse, I gladly commend to your Lordship, desiring that under the wings of your honorable protection it may walk safely abroad in the world, to the view and benefit of others. The true report of your long-standing love of the truth, and of your favorable inclination toward its ministers and dispensers, has encouraged me in this; all the more because the subject fits in some measure your present state. For God has adorned your house with olive plants,#footnote[Ps 128:3.] a virtuous and hopeful offspring, whose young years, seasoned in it by the sweet savor of their father’s virtues, may in time yield a plentiful increase of honor in this commonwealth, and so advance your name in themselves and their posterity. To this may be added my own desire to give some testimony of duty to your Lordship, holding it your due to be held in honorable esteem among men, since your care has been to maintain the honor of the Most High by your constant profession and practice of religion. And so, emboldened by the persuasion of your Lordship’s favor and acceptance, I humbly take my leave, and commend you to the grace of#footnote[Acts 20:32] God, who is able to build you up further and give you an inheritance among those who are sanctified. #emph[Finchingfield,] September 26, 1609.

